  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1259 PM EDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nIncreasing southwesterly flow has allowed a return to temperatures \nin the 70s with some increase in near surface (950 mb and lower per \n12Z KILX sounding). Further increase in moisture will await the \narrival of a cold front and a narrow swath of increased isentropic \nlift and lower CPDs to bring some potential for isolated to \nscattered showers (maybe a storm) Thursday. Greatest lift seems to \nexist during the afternoon, which would coincide with peak heating \n(although clouds may hamper that to some extent). Toned down likely \npops in NW to higher chance for now. \n\nThe passage of the front will bring a push of CAA, essentially \ncutting off 10 degrees or so from the high temps we have enjoyed \nover the past several days. This is still above normal for the end \nof Oct. Sfc high pressure will build across the Great Lakes, keeping \nthe warm and moist conditions just to our south through the upcoming \nweekend. Depending on the final resting place of the stationary \nfront and fluctuations in its location, some chance for showers does \nremain in this period. By no means is there expected to be a washout \nand Halloween activities appear to be mostly dry.\n\nModels are in agreement on an upper low moving to the Pacific NW \nshores by 18Z Sat. From here differences increase as a series on \nnorthern and southern stream waves either eject from this upper low \nor move into the SW US. GFS remains the most aggressive with a deeper \nupper low over OK by 6Z Wed, vs the EC/GEM that have a similar \nlocation but much weaker. Suffice to say, plenty of moisture will \npush north with this system but the timing of specific impacts to \nour area remain in doubt at this point.",
